# Depot BuildGo

Rules for using the buildGo Nix functionality to build Go programs and packages.

<rule>
name: buildgo_usage
description: Standards for using buildGo to compile Go programs and packages
filters:
  - type: file_path
    pattern: ".*\\.nix$"
  - type: content
    pattern: "buildGo"

actions:
  - type: reject
    conditions:
      - pattern: "import .*nix/buildGo"
        message: "Do not import buildGo directly. Use depot.nix.buildGo instead"

  - type: suggest
    message: |
      Follow these conventions when using buildGo:

      1. Use buildGo via depot.nix:
         ```nix
         { depot, pkgs, ... }:

         depot.nix.buildGo.program {
           name = "program-name";
           ...
         }
         ```

      2. For building executable programs:
         ```nix
         depot.nix.buildGo.program {
           name = "program-name";  # Name of the output binary
           srcs = [
             ./main.go
             ./other.go
           ];
           deps = [ ];  # List of Go package dependencies
         }
         ```

      3. For building libraries/packages:
         ```nix
         depot.nix.buildGo.package {
           name = "package-name";
           path = "full/import/path";  # Go import path
           srcs = [ ./package.go ];
         }
         ```

      4. For external dependencies, fetch and build them as packages:
         ```nix
         let
           myDep = depot.nix.buildGo.package {
             name = "dep-name";
             path = "github.com/org/repo/pkg";
             srcs = let
               src = pkgs.fetchFromGitHub {
                 owner = "org";
                 repo = "repo";
                 rev = "v1.2.3";  # Use specific version
                 sha256 = "...";  # Get hash with nix-prefetch-url
               };
             in [
               # List all required .go files, excluding tests
               "${src}/pkg/file1.go"
               "${src}/pkg/file2.go"
             ];
           };
         in ...
         ```

      5. When specifying external dependencies:
         - Always pin to specific versions using git tags or commit hashes
         - Include all necessary source files (excluding tests)
         - Use nix-prefetch-url to get correct SHA256 hashes
         - Consider compatibility with the Go version being used

      6. For version compatibility issues:
         - Try older versions of dependencies if newer ones require unavailable Go features
         - Check the Go version provided by buildGo using:
           ```nix
           depot.nix.buildGo.go.version
           ```

examples:
  - input: |
      # Bad: Importing buildGo directly
      let
        buildGo = import ../../nix/buildGo { inherit pkgs; };
      in
      buildGo.program { ... }

      # Good: Using via depot.nix
      { depot, pkgs, ... }:
      depot.nix.buildGo.program { ... }
    output: "Correctly using buildGo via depot.nix"

  - input: |
      # Program with external dependencies
      { depot, pkgs, ... }:

      let
        myDep = depot.nix.buildGo.package {
          name = "dep";
          path = "github.com/org/repo";
          srcs = let
            src = pkgs.fetchFromGitHub {
              owner = "org";
              repo = "repo";
              rev = "v1.0.0";
              sha256 = "...";
            };
          in [ "${src}/pkg.go" ];
        };
      in
      depot.nix.buildGo.program {
        name = "app";
        srcs = [ ./main.go ];
        deps = [ myDep ];
      }
    output: "Go program with external dependency using depot.nix"

metadata:
  priority: high
  version: 1.0
</rule>

This rule provides guidelines for:
1. Using buildGo via depot.nix
2. Building programs vs packages
3. Handling external dependencies
4. Version compatibility
5. Best practices for dependency management

